To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function or the `return` statement.

The `exit()` function is used to exit the entire program immediately and return control to the operating system. It takes an integer argument that specifies the status code of the termination:

```c
int main() {
    // some code here
    exit(0);  // normal termination with status code 0
}
```

If you want to terminate the program abruptly without returning any value, you can use the `abort()` function instead:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // some code here
    abort();  // abrupt termination
}
```

Alternatively, if you want to return from the current function but not necessarily exit the program, you can use the `return` statement:

```c
int main() {
    int result = some_function();
    return result;  // return from the current function
}
```

Remember that using `exit()` or `abort()` will cause the program to terminate immediately, so it's generally better to use them only in situations where you need to force the program to end quickly.

To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function or the `return` statement.

The `exit()` function is used to exit the program immediately and return control to the operating system. It takes an integer argument that specifies the status code of the termination:

```c
int main() {
    // some code here

    exit(0);  // normal termination with no error
}
```

If you want to specify an error condition for the termination, you can pass -1 as the argument:

```c
int main() {
    // some code here

    exit(-1);  // abnormal termination with error
}
```

Alternatively, you can use the `return` statement to exit the function early if certain conditions are met. This will also cause the program to terminate:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 5;

    if (i == 3) {
        return;  // exits the function immediately
    }

    printf("Hello World\n");
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the program will print "Hello World" before exiting due to the `return` statement.

To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function or the `return` statement.

The `exit()` function is used to exit the program immediately and return control to the operating system. It takes an integer argument that specifies the status code of the termination:

```c
int main() {
    // some code here
    exit(0);  // normal termination with status code 0
}
```

If you want to terminate the program without returning any value (i.e., without calling any functions), you can use the `return` statement:

```c
int main() {
    // some code here
    return;  // terminates the program without returning anything
}
```

In both cases, after the program has terminated, it will be returned to the operating system where it was originally called from.

